 lessons  most people are obsessed with the idea  of finding advanced tactics quick fixes  everything that needs to be advanced  advanced but the truth is advanced  tactics will only give you incremental  improvements compared to mastery of the  basics in anything if you look at  cause-and-effect the basics probably  count for 80 to 90 percent of your  improvement while any advanced tactics  probably 10 or 20 percent not only that  they're much harder to learn as well so  if you're not getting the results you  want I can assure you that the answer is  not hiding in advanced tactics it's  hiding in the fundamentals of what  you're doing if there's one principle  behind all martial arts improvement I  would say it's the analysis and the  removal of what's unnecessary it's  obviously what every kind of biohacking  modality promises us a better way to do  things faster more efficient in less  time and so on or good technique is a  result of lack of refinement and more  energy is being used than is necessary  to complete a technique which is  consequently wasted this can be overcome  back correctly understanding one's  bodily mechanics this series will focus  on those concepts with regards to kick  in technique so you want to work towards  been able to execute your kicks with  maximum efficiency devastating power  while also being graceful and having  finesse hence the reason for me calling  my teaching and my brand optimal  technique it's this constant analysis  refinement and removing what's  unnecessary that allows the master of  any endeavor to perform effortlessly in  a way that the majority of people think  is impossible at its foundation this is  just an incredibly refined economical  nature  so the first concept that we're going to  discuss is a term I like to call the arc  of attack so this applies to any kicks  that are coming in from the side so like  a turning kick or a hook kick for  instance what you want to do with this  is obviously you've got your center line  down the middle and wherever your target  is striking this down that center line  you want to imagine an arc shape like  this which is where your kick is going  to travel across now people who aren't  very experienced with kicks and don't  really know what they're doing will have  a big wide arc and that's where they're  wasting all their energy because the  kick has to go fully across in life  whatever we do compounds over time and  by constantly practicing and drilling on  those basic concepts that's going to  have an effect and carry over into all  different areas of your training so take  a look at this example to see what I'm  talking about if you can see here I am  performing a standing and hook kick from  my lead leg now if you look at it  closely we've got the chamber the  extension point and then finally the  reach aim bur at the end which completes  the kick now this is roughly the shape  that the kick travels through on its  part with the three white dots  representing the different points now  this is what it would look like if it  traveled across a much wider arc and  this is where the energy is being wasted  the why did the arc of the kick the more  the leg has to do therefore wasting  energy and resulting in a slower kick  and the reason that's really important  is because then when you go to do say a  spin hook keep like I'm doing here then  the practice of having the short arc on  the prerequisite kick which was the  standard hook kick is gonna then  translate into this kick as well and how  that would work in Reverse is if you had  a very wide and loose arc on your normal  hook kick then you would also have a  very wide and loose spin hook kick so in  order to shorten the arc like this we  need to be aware of a few things  if you imagine here this is the point  where you're chambered kick extends  before you hit your target you travel  across through and hit the target and  then this point is where you reach aim  bur the kick back in kicking technique  should be precise and not labored the  legs are some of the largest muscles in  the body so we need to learn to control  them properly or they're just going to  SAP all of our energy so by focusing on  this we're not having to muscle through  and drag our legs through the technique  which will result in a slow kick with  lackluster power and almost as important  as energy conservation refining your arc  will serve to make your kicks more  invisible against it  as well as I mentioned before if you  have a big wide arc like this and you'll  having to drag your leg right through a  technique then your opponent's going to  see that coming a mile off it's like if  I was to throw a punch for instance for  this example if I swing a punch out like  this you're gonna see it coming a mile  off whereas if I'm nice and tight and  throw it in it's a lot harder to read so  if you've got a nice narrow arc it will  almost appear as if even though you're  doing a turning kick then it comes  straight towards your opponent and then  only travels across in a short motion  before it's returned back now our eyes  struggle to judge distances correctly in  front of us and we're not really able to  read that we're much better with  peripheral vision which is why we have  brake lights on our cars so this isn't  such an issue when you're executing  techniques to travel down a straight  line like a front kick or a side kick  for example but if you are unaware of  this and you're very wide when you are  throwing say a turning kick then even in  the ponent with the most limited  experience is going to be able to read  that a mile off by understanding and  working within your arc of attack you  can make it so that your side on kicking  techniques aren't visible until it's too  late  this is one of the reasons that Edson  Barboza was able to learn this  spectacular spin hook kick knockout if  you notice how tightly wrapped his body  is when he's spinning round his opponent  didn't even see it coming and just had  no chance and then combine that as well  if the opponent's moving forward it's  even harder to spot be mindful to take  this concept into your own training and  build up slowly at first so start slowly  practicing it slowly taking your body  exactly through the movement you want to  do and then when it comes to doing it  quickly hopefully it will be second  nature by then and you'll be able to  execute the technique exactly the same  [Music]  so a good way to drill this as mentioned  before is by practicing slowly I'm gonna  hold onto this ledge here this wall and  you want to you want to do this so that  you are taking care of your balance so  that you can completely give your  attention and focus to what you want to  drill into your muscles if you were  practicing this and you weren't holding  on to something for instance and you  were trying to kick your your balance  then you're not going to really be able  to focus on what you want so that's why  we do this firstly obviously ensure that  you're pivoting foot is in the correct  position or that's gonna mess you up  straightaway  make sure the pivoting before my heel is  facing completely towards the camera now  it's out at the minute but that's where  it is so that I can open my hips up  correctly and in terms of the arc then  for this I'm gonna show you a couple of  different demonstrations the way I kick  like this is I will have the chamber  position up here and out like I said  I'll try to extend the kick as close to  the target as I can so I want to bring  it out about here first doing a hook  kick for instance and I'll bring it  across the target and bring it back down  straight away so if I do that quickly it  will look like this and again and once  more now if you may see somebody less  experienced do this their kick is gonna  come out wider from here and then finish  more across there which is what I was  talking about using too much energy and  wasting time and being completely  readable as well so the way that would  look would be more like this would be  more wide lack of a chamber slow out of  control the body is falling down as  opposed to be a nice and tight  whippy and back in control and coming  down again another way to really help  this and boost it a little bit is to  practice the visualization technique  that I laid out in my previous video  visualization is really good for things  like teaching your body how you would  like it to move beforehand so I will put  a link in the description for that as  well what's up guys I want to hear from  martial eyes so that I can make my own  line material better please feel free to  send me an email at this address I'd  like to ask you a few questions if you  liked this video and found value in it  please subscribe to the Baniya and  follow me across all social media  platforms thank you very much for  watching and I'll see you next time

